  {"comment": "SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT. GitHub #635's design (mitcad#2365; opened with --open, never committed; tools/cli/fillet-counterbore-seam-reported-test.cmake): Extrude1 (F2) a block 36 x 40 x 20 (x 0..36, y -20..20, z -20..0); Chamfer1 (F3) its bottom along x = 36, 30 on the bottom and 5 up the side (the chamfer from (6, y, -20) to (36, y, -15)); Hole1 (F5) two counterbores from the bottom at (16, -11.5) and (16, 11.5), 8.5 through, 15 x 8 deep, both walls' seams towards +y; Fillet9 (F16) rounds with 2 the edges of the end faces y = 20 (end) and y = -20 (start) with the chamfer and with the bottom. Each counterbore's rim on the chamfer comes 1 mm from its end face: the rounding's contact on the chamfer, 2 from the edge, passes over the counterbore, and the counterbore's wall cuts the rounding there (the bump on the pocket's side). The body is the mirror image of itself in y = 0, so each end rounds as the other. 22199.734817 before Fillet9."},
  {"comment": "Before (mitcad#2365): at the end y = 20 the counterbore's seam runs up from its rim under the rounding, and the rounding was walked from the end face over to the counterbore's wall instead: 1.22 mm3 more material there, the end face's edge with the rounding bent down beside the hole (573.2145 instead of 572.1701) and two more faces (B-spline ones); 22139.050701 for the design's set. Now both ends as the start y = -20 was."},
  {"comment": "The design's own Fillet9, all four edges: one valid solid without a note; each counterbore's wall cut alike (297.3431, 298.4513 before), the end and start faces alike, no walked face."},
